{{!-- docs/mobile-bar.hbs — the way into the docs tree on a phone.

     The same problem the lesson player solved, so the same answer: the
     system's `.ns-panelbar` says where you are, and one control opens the
     whole tree as a `.ns-lessonmodal` popover.

     A POPOVER, so this needs NO JavaScript. The platform supplies the top
     layer, light dismiss and Escape. The theme's version was an Alpine
     dropdown with its own `dopen` state, its own click-outside handler and a
     hand-rotated chevron — all of which the platform does for free.

     Hidden at lg and up by the component, where the sticky .ns-sidebar is the
     real navigation.

     Param: label — the current article, shown in the bar. --}}
<div class="ns-panelbar ns-docs__bar">
  <span class="ns-panelbar__now">
    <span class="ns-panelbar__kicker">{{t "Documentation"}}</span>
    <span class="ns-panelbar__title">{{#if label}}{{label}}{{else}}{{t "Browse the docs"}}{{/if}}</span>
  </span>
  <button type="button" class="ns-btn ns-btn--outline ns-btn--sm" popovertarget="docs-tree">
    {{> icons name="list"}}{{t "Contents"}}
  </button>
</div>

<div class="ns-lessonmodal" id="docs-tree" popover>
  <div class="ns-lessonmodal__head">
    <span class="ns-lessonmodal__title">{{t "Documentation"}}</span>
    <button class="ns-btn ns-btn--quiet ns-btn--icon ns-btn--sm" type="button"
            popovertarget="docs-tree" popovertargetaction="hide" aria-label="{{t "Close"}}">{{> icons name="x"}}</button>
  </div>
  <div class="ns-lessonmodal__list">
    <a class="ns-sidebar__link" href="/docs/">{{> icons name="books"}}{{t "Docs home"}}</a>
    {{> "docs/sections" view="nav"}}
  </div>
</div>
